\paragraph{Fast Johnson--Lindenstrauss Transform.} A random orthogonal projection preserves Euclidean norms in expectation. The FJLT realises such a projection with three inexpensive operations: element-wise sign flips ($D$), a Walsh--Hadamard transform ($H$) and a fixed random permutation ($\pi$). Retaining only the first $k$ coordinates after $\pi$ yields a sketch $z\in\mathbb{R}^k$. The transform costs $O(d\log d)$ and stores no weights.  
                       ^^
Warning 8 in paper.tex line 89: Wrong length of dash may have been used.
\paragraph{Fast Johnson--Lindenstrauss Transform.} A random orthogonal projection preserves Euclidean norms in expectation. The FJLT realises such a projection with three inexpensive operations: element-wise sign flips ($D$), a Walsh--Hadamard transform ($H$) and a fixed random permutation ($\pi$). Retaining only the first $k$ coordinates after $\pi$ yields a sketch $z\in\mathbb{R}^k$. The transform costs $O(d\log d)$ and stores no weights.  
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         ^^
